WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.339 --> 00:00:04.770
From behind the scenes bon mots to how the
film might even be connected to Get Out and

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:09.180
early prognosticating for its Oscar chances,
here's what you need to know before you see

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:13.260
acclaimed filmmaker Jordan Peele's 2019 horror
outing, Us.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:18.900
After getting his start on the sketch comedy
series Mad TV, where he spent five seasons,

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:23.140
it might have seemed unlikely that Jordan
Peele would soon shoot to the upper echelon

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:28.560
of filmmakers and earn himself an Academy
Award, but once audiences got to see his whip-smart

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:33.520
social satire on Key &amp; Peele, the hit sketch
show starring Peele and his comedic scene

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:37.800
partner Keegan Michael Key, they may have
realized what a bright future Peele had ahead

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:38.300
of him.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:43.780
From his pitch-perfect Obama impression to
his enthusiastic valet, Peele quickly made

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:47.340
his mark on sketch comedy before setting his
sights even higher.

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:51.140
"Mmmm, baked to perfection.

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:52.740
Can you believe it?"

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:59.020
Peele's 2017 film Get Out, chronicling an
extremely bad weekend away for Chris at his

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:03.360
girlfriend's family's country house, ended
up making its mark as the most profitable

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:08.400
film of the year as well as netting an Academy
Award for Best Original Screenplay for Peele

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:11.900
along with nominations for Best Director and
Best Picture.

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:17.399
Peele has since retired from acting, noting
that he prefers directing, but word is that

00:01:17.399 --> 00:01:22.109
he hasn't changed despite moving behind the
camera he brought his Obama impression back,

00:01:22.109 --> 00:01:24.980
among others, to lighten the mood on set during
Get Out.

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:30.419
After Get Out, which paired veteran actors
like Bradley Whitford and Catherine Keener

00:01:30.419 --> 00:01:35.049
alongside lesser-known performers like Daniel
Kaluuya and Allison Williams, Peele had to

00:01:35.049 --> 00:01:39.399
assemble a noteworthy cast for his second
film, and he certainly didn't disappoint.

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:44.320
Two of the first names announced were Winston
Duke and Lupita Nyong'o, the former of whom

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:48.780
is known for roles in Person of Interest and
other small film and television roles while

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:53.579
the latter made an unforgettable debut in
Steve McQueen's 12 Years a Slave while she

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:58.100
was still attending Yale Drama School and
even won an Academy Award for her work.

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:04.209
The two actors also notably appeared together
in 2018's Black Panther, the juggernaut Marvel

00:02:04.209 --> 00:02:09.110
superhero film starring Chadwick Boseman and
Michael B. Jordan that crushed box office

00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:09.600
records.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:15.400
Aside from Duke and Nyong'o, the film features
two other stars familiar to TV fans: Elisabeth

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:19.640
Moss and Tim Heidecker, who are known for
vastly different projects.

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:25.480
Elisabeth Moss rose to fame on serious prestige
dramas like Mad Men and The Handmaid's Tale,

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:29.920
while Heidecker's biggest claim to fame is
his work on the Adult Swim original Tim and

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:31.840
Eric Awesome Show, Great Job!

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:33.880
"Hi me momo Tim Heidecker…

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:35.640
And welcome to discount prices!

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:39.400
Cheap cheap cheap,"

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:44.600
After Get Out, which was a completely original
concept for a "social thriller," many fans

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:48.580
probably expected Peele to produce a new and
completely original concept.

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:53.340
But it's important to recognize the genesis
of Us came from one of the best works of horror

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:53.920
in history.

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:59.200
When Peele was only a kid, he watched a 1960
episode of The Twilight Zone called "Mirror

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:04.900
Image," about a woman waiting for a bus on
a rainy evening who spots her exact duplicate

00:03:04.909 --> 00:03:08.209
after a series of odd and disconcerting events.

00:03:08.209 --> 00:03:12.030
Convinced that her double is sent from an
alternate universe to replace her, the woman

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:17.580
begins to unravel and is eventually carted
away, even though her double is all too real.

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:22.480
This isn't Peele's only brush with the famous
horror anthology series he's producing and

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:27.560
hosting the next generation of The Twilight
Zone, which will air on CBS in 2019.

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:31.780
Though there have been a few Twilight Zone
reboots since the original series aired, this

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:34.220
one is perhaps the most highly anticipated.

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:40.319
Since Us is following one of the most beloved
films of 2017, it's natural that fans would

00:03:40.319 --> 00:03:44.609
want to find connections between the two,
and theories have run rampant as to how they

00:03:44.609 --> 00:03:45.600
might be linked.

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:50.420
An obvious connection is how Peele plays with
issues of the subconscious, from the stolen

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:56.019
bodies in Get Out to the mysterious doubles
in Us representing a stolen or "other" consciousness

00:03:56.020 --> 00:03:57.320
taken from the protagonists.

00:03:57.709 --> 00:04:02.800
The trailer for Us seems to feature some pretty
overt similarities to Get Out as well, including

00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:08.209
plenty of shots of driving as well as a straight-on
shot of a tear-soaked Nyong'o that clearly

00:04:08.209 --> 00:04:10.340
echoes the most famous shot of Get Out.

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:15.219
From there, the theories get pretty intense,
from the importance of scissors as it pertains

00:04:15.219 --> 00:04:20.560
to "severing" links within one's soul to the
possibility that the family in Us has undergone

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:22.480
the central surgery from Get Out.

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:27.260
It's all pretty wild, but considering that
Peele once told a journalist he wanted to

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:31.740
"weave in the universe" of Get Out into future
films, maybe nothing is off limits.

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:35.720
Get Out was a perfect entry into horror for
many viewers.

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:40.400
Despite just a few jump scares and plenty
of gore for good measure, the film relied

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:44.780
heavily on emotional and psychological trauma
instead of traditional horror, and was even

00:04:44.780 --> 00:04:45.780
funny at times.

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:50.720
But in the end, it wasn't meant as a classic
horror film, with Peele himself calling it

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:54.920
a "social thriller" in the vein of Alfred
Hitchcock rather than a true horror movie.

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:58.040
"I mean, how you gonna scare a couple of adults
with a puddle"

00:04:58.140 --> 00:04:58.900
"Stupid, stupid."

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:07.400
Every early report on Us, however, has noted
its full leap into horror after early screenings,

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:11.509
critics and fans flocked to the internet to
tell the world that Us is much scarier than

00:05:11.509 --> 00:05:12.000
Get Out.

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:16.960
Genre fans should be excited for this next
chapter in Peele's filmography but for those

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:20.400
who love Peele's offbeat sense of humor, there's
no need to worry.

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:25.020
Reports from screenings note that audiences
were often roaring with laughter, and thanks

00:05:25.030 --> 00:05:30.050
to Peele's sharp comedic timing mixed with
his incisive social statements, Us is sure

00:05:30.050 --> 00:05:33.220
to deliver as much of a gut-punching message
as its predecessor.

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:39.440
Peele's second film has been hotly anticipated
since Get Out conquered the box office, and

00:05:39.449 --> 00:05:43.979
after he announced in February of 2018 that
he would be working on a second film, fans

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:46.340
of horror and Key &amp; Peele alike have been
waiting.

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:52.520
Though the wide release of the film was originally
supposed to take place on March 15, 2019,

00:05:52.520 --> 00:05:57.720
the producers and Peele wisely bumped it back
to March 22 after Us landed an opening night

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:02.940
slot at the South by Southwest film festival,
where it screened to overwhelming acclaim.

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:07.440
Reactions were universally adoring for the
frightening film, with fans noting that it

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:12.639
was everything from terrifying to gorgeous
to stunningly acted, driving up enthusiasm

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:15.240
for those who didn't get the chance to check
out Us in Austin.

00:06:15.980 --> 00:06:19.440
Jordan Peele broke several Oscar barriers
with his debut film.

00:06:19.750 --> 00:06:23.980
He walked away with the statue for Best Original
Screenplay, making history as the first black

00:06:23.980 --> 00:06:25.960
screenwriter to ever win the award.

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:30.220
With this highly pedigreed background, it
seems all but inevitable that Us will be a

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:35.520
serious contender in the next awards season,
but thanks to its genre, that isn't guaranteed.

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:40.660
Only six horror films have ever been nominated
for Best Picture, and horror films and the

00:06:40.660 --> 00:06:43.440
performances within are frequently overlooked.

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:48.250
With plenty of Oscar veterans in the cast,
Us may stand a chance, but it remains to be

00:06:48.250 --> 00:06:52.640
seen whether Academy voters will let horror
films back into the fray so soon after Get

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:53.140
Out.

00:06:53.540 --> 00:06:58.320
When fans and reviewers start wondering about
Us' Oscar chances, the first mentions will

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:00.860
likely go to Nyong'o's leading performance.

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:06.100
Since winning an Oscar, the star has kept
plenty busy, appearing on Broadway in Eclipsed,

00:07:06.100 --> 00:07:10.400
as well as joining the Star Wars universe
as motion-capture character Maz Kanata . Her

00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:16.200
biggest post-Oscar project, however, was Black
Panther, in which she appeared as Nakia, a

00:07:16.210 --> 00:07:21.050
Wakandan spy and love interest to Chadwick
Boseman's King T'Challa, joining the Marvel

00:07:21.050 --> 00:07:24.200
Cinematic Universe in one of its highest-grossing
films yet.

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:29.100
It should come as no surprise, then, that
Nyong'o is being singled out for Us.

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:33.900
Along with the rest of the main cast, she
plays two parts Adelaide, a wife and mother,

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:37.500
as well as Adelaide's terrifying double, known
only as "Red."

00:07:38.360 --> 00:07:42.840
New York Magazine describes Nyong'o's performance
as her own doppelgänger as "an achievement

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:47.660
on another level," while the Los Angeles Times
notes that the film "belongs to Nyong'o."

00:07:48.780 --> 00:07:53.620
With a modest budget of $4.5 million, Get
Out would have been a success at the box office

00:07:53.620 --> 00:08:00.120
even if it had a completely average theatrical
run, but Peele's debut grossed $255 million

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:06.800
worldwide with a net profit of over $120 million,
making it the tenth most profitable film of

00:08:06.810 --> 00:08:12.110
2017 as well as earning the distinction of
being the highest-grossing original screenplay

00:08:12.110 --> 00:08:13.860
since The Blair Witch Project.

00:08:14.020 --> 00:08:18.460
Thanks to Get Out's success, Peele was given
much more budgetary breathing room for his

00:08:18.460 --> 00:08:18.960
follow-up.

00:08:19.340 --> 00:08:23.900
Peele wanted more financial flexibility, and
thanks to his established success as well

00:08:23.900 --> 00:08:28.500
as the first-look deal that Universal made
with his production company, Monkeypaw Productions,

00:08:28.870 --> 00:08:31.920
he was afforded a $20 million budget for Us.

00:08:31.930 --> 00:08:36.300
By increasing the budget but still keeping
it low enough that the studio would be unlikely

00:08:36.300 --> 00:08:40.680
to interfere with the finished product, Peele
walked a difficult line to make sure he could

00:08:40.680 --> 00:08:42.680
make Us exactly as he planned.

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:47.890
Beneath the horror and the humor that make
up the backbone of Peele's filmography lies

00:08:47.890 --> 00:08:53.260
a foundation of involved, complex messages,
and between Get Out and Us, the director and

00:08:53.260 --> 00:08:55.320
writer has a lot to say about America.

00:08:55.730 --> 00:09:01.560
In Get Out, Peele infused a question of self-examination,
and with all this in mind, it made it even

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:05.900
more satisfying for viewers to watch Chris
escape at the end of the film, knowing that

00:09:05.900 --> 00:09:08.840
he could remain himself and keep ownership
of his body.

00:09:09.380 --> 00:09:12.600
"Consider this situation f------- handled."

00:09:13.220 --> 00:09:18.120
Peele and the cast of Us have kept details
fairly close to the vest, remaining vague

00:09:18.130 --> 00:09:23.020
about specific plot points, but Peele has
been clear about his intentions, noting that

00:09:23.020 --> 00:09:27.590
when groups or individuals don't take clear
looks at themselves both as people and as

00:09:27.590 --> 00:09:31.380
Americans evil is given the chance to survive
and thrive.

00:09:32.130 --> 00:09:36.140
With "The Tethered," the doppelgängers in
the film, Peele can express both an internal

00:09:36.140 --> 00:09:40.500
and wide-reaching fear of "others," making
viewers really think as they leave the theater.

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:45.960
Besides being a box office smash, Get Out
was a critical hit and cultural phenomenon

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:50.280
upon its release, even inspiring a college
course on racism and horror.

00:09:50.580 --> 00:09:55.520
Like its predecessor, Us racked up plenty
of raves before it saw wide release, following

00:09:55.520 --> 00:10:00.770
in Get Out's footsteps to earn an initial
100% on Rotten Tomatoes after its hit opening

00:10:00.770 --> 00:10:02.960
at South by Southwest in early March.

00:10:03.260 --> 00:10:07.340
If audiences were worried about a "sophomore
slump" from Peele, this doesn't seem to be

00:10:07.340 --> 00:10:12.070
the case, and though the score may dip as
more reviews come in, this stunning critical

00:10:12.070 --> 00:10:16.410
early showing is still a strong indicator
for the film's future reception and its potential

00:10:16.410 --> 00:10:19.810
box office earnings, adding another success
to Peele's career.

00:10:20.840 --> 00:10:25.320
Jordan Peele is a self-professed lifelong
horror fan, so it shouldn't be surprising

00:10:25.320 --> 00:10:30.010
that he found his way into the genre, even
if he did get his start in side-splitting

00:10:30.010 --> 00:10:31.260
sketch comedy shows.

00:10:31.430 --> 00:10:35.680
For Get Out, Peele noted ahead of the film's
release that he took inspiration from everything

00:10:35.680 --> 00:10:40.000
from classic films like Rosemary's Baby and
The Stepford Wives when it came to informing

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:45.160
tone and the building of tension but considering
that Us is reportedly much more classically

00:10:45.160 --> 00:10:50.760
scary than Get Out, it stands to reason that
Peele would have an entirely new list of inspirations.

00:10:51.060 --> 00:10:55.580
Peele revealed his "reading list" of sorts
well before Us' release, and even told star

00:10:55.680 --> 00:11:00.570
Lupita Nyong'o to watch ten specific films
so that they could have a "shared language"

00:11:00.570 --> 00:11:01.060
on set.

00:11:01.280 --> 00:11:05.700
Between classics like The Birds and The Shining,
modern hits like The Babadook, and foreign

00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:10.560
films like Let the Right One In, this varied
and diverse list is the perfect primer for

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:13.480
any horror fan that wants to brush up before
Us.

